WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.046
You tap play on Anti-Hero.

00:00:07.046 --> 00:00:12.093
That one click sets off a chain reaction through every algorithm in this course.

00:00:12.093 --> 00:00:17.139
Three systems touch the path, each tuned for the opposite of the other two, and the whole thing finishes in about two seconds.

00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:22.561
Here is the whole journey on one canvas.

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:27.983
The tap fans out into three systems in sequence.

00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:33.404
Act one is the OLTP click.

00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:38.826
The play appends to an LSM tree.

00:00:38.826 --> 00:00:44.248
It hits an in-memory memtable, the red block in RAM, returns in about eight milliseconds, and flushes to an SS Table on disk later.

00:00:44.248 --> 00:00:49.670
No random disk IO on the hot path, which is how one node sustains a hundred thousand writes a second.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:55.092
Act two is the ETL.

00:00:55.092 --> 00:01:00.514
At two AM, Spark hash-partitions a day of events across a thousand nodes, BigSorts each partition on timestamp, broadcast-joins the small dimension tables, and writes the result columnar as Parquet.

00:01:00.514 --> 00:01:05.936
One hundred terabytes shrink to ten.

00:01:05.936 --> 00:01:11.357
Act three is the OLAP query.

00:01:11.357 --> 00:01:16.779
The dashboard scans that columnar warehouse, reading three of twenty columns, and answers in two seconds.

00:01:16.779 --> 00:01:22.201
Every block on this path is a primitive you built earlier, now wired end to end.

00:01:22.201 --> 00:01:27.950
Look closer at act one.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.699
You tap play, and an INSERT INTO listens lands in about eight milliseconds.

00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:39.449
The write goes into the memtable in RAM, returns instantly, and flushes to an SS Table on disk later as a batch.

00:01:39.449 --> 00:01:45.198
No random disk IO on the hot path.

00:01:45.198 --> 00:01:50.947
That is the LSM tree from Module Three B, and it is why Spotify absorbs eight point six billion events a day without blinking.

00:01:50.947 --> 00:01:55.604
Act two reshapes a whole day at once.

00:01:55.604 --> 00:02:00.262
At two AM Spark takes yesterday's eight point six billion events and runs four steps you already know.

00:02:00.262 --> 00:02:04.919
Repartition by user id spreads them across a thousand nodes, the hash partitioning.

00:02:04.919 --> 00:02:09.576
Sort within each partition on timestamp, the BigSort.

00:02:09.576 --> 00:02:14.233
Broadcast the small dimension tables to every node, the broadcast join.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:18.891
Then write columnar Parquet, which compresses a hundred terabytes down to ten.

00:02:18.891 --> 00:02:23.548
Three hours on a thousand-node cluster.

00:02:23.548 --> 00:02:28.371
Act three is the payoff.

00:02:28.371 --> 00:02:33.194
The CEO opens the app and the Top Artists by Country dashboard renders in two seconds.

00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:38.017
It is a star-schema join.

00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:42.841
The dim artists table is ten megabytes, broadcast to all thousand nodes, so no shuffle.

00:02:42.841 --> 00:02:47.664
The fact plays table is one petabyte, scanned columnar.

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:52.487
Only artist id, country, and date are reed off disk, three columns out of twenty.

00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:57.310
A two-second query against a petabyte warehouse, on a broadcast join plus a columnar scan.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.981
Eight algorithms, three systems, one click.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:06.651
Act one needs blazing writes, so the click lands in an LSM tree with no random IO.

00:03:06.651 --> 00:03:11.322
Act three needs blazing scans, so the dashboard reads compressed columnar storage.

00:03:11.322 --> 00:03:15.992
Act two is the nightly conversion between them.

00:03:15.992 --> 00:03:20.663
You never pick one structure.

00:03:20.663 --> 00:03:25.333
You pick the right one for each stage, and the optimizer is the glue that decides which runs where.

00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:30.004
That is how a billion clicks a day become a two-second dashboard.
